<p>McAllen Public Adjuster serves McAllen, Hidalgo County, and surrounding Rio Grande Valley (RGV) communities. We do not operate storefront offices in each city; we come to the damaged property, inspect it, and handle the claim from there. The sections below describe the areas we cover and the kinds of weather each has actually recorded, based on the NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) Storm Events Database.</p>
<p>Want to see reported events near a specific address? Search any city or ZIP code with our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a>, or browse <a href="/storm-history/">Hidalgo County storm history</a>.</p>

<h2>McAllen: our primary service area</h2>
<p>McAllen is where most of our work happens. The city has seen some of the most severe storm reports in the county. On April 20, 2012, the public reported <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=379500" target="_blank" rel="noopener">softball-size (4.5-inch) hail at McAllen High School</a>, with golf ball to baseball hail falling for more than twenty minutes across McAllen, Mission, and Pharr. On April 28, 2023, NWS storm surveys estimated gusts near 85 mph and found <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1084452" target="_blank" rel="noopener">more than half the shingles stripped from a two-story building on S. 29th Street</a>. In May 2025, a supercell produced hail up to 3 inches in north McAllen.</p>
<p>Not every McAllen claim starts with hail. On July 16, 2007, an <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=48137" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EF1 tornado and torrential rain</a> brought widespread street flooding to the city, and during the February 2021 freeze McAllen International Airport recorded a low of 21&deg;F, conditions that can lead to burst pipes and interior water damage. See our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page for how those losses are typically handled.</p>
<p>McAllen's housing is largely slab-on-grade homes with composition shingle roofs and stucco or brick veneer, and its commercial corridors include many metal and low-slope roofs. We handle <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail</a>, <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind</a>, <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof</a>, fire, and water claims across the city. Repairs here must meet the City's adopted 2024 codes; see our <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen building codes guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Communities we serve across the RGV</h2>

<h3>Mission</h3>
<p>Mission sits just west of McAllen and shares much of its storm exposure. NCEI lists a <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=5593660" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4.5-inch hail report in Mission on March 14, 1997</a>, and during the April 21, 2023 hailstorm residents reported stones up to 2.5 inches. A May 31, 2016 downburst first damaged trees and weaker roofs in the Mission/Palmview area before intensifying farther east. Hail claims involving roofs, vehicles, and HVAC units are common requests from Mission homeowners.</p>

<h3>Edinburg</h3>
<p>Edinburg, north of McAllen, has a long record of severe hail. On March 29, 2012, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=373680" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2.75-inch hail was reported near Edinburg Municipal Airport</a>, with up to an hour of continuous wind-driven hail breaking north-facing windows. On May 8, 2025, a microburst hit north McAllen near the Edinburg city line. Wind-driven hail often damages siding, windows, and screens as well as roofs, so we document all elevations, not just the roof.</p>

<h3>Pharr</h3>
<p>Pharr, east of McAllen, was within the swath of golf ball to baseball hail on April 20, 2012, and NWS surveys documented damage in Pharr after the April 28, 2023 wind event. Pharr includes a mix of residential neighborhoods and commercial and industrial properties, so we see both <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential</a> and <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial claims</a> here.</p>

<h3>San Juan</h3>
<p>San Juan was among the communities with reported damage from the April 28, 2023 overnight wind event. Wind claims often involve lifted or missing shingles, damaged fences, and fallen trees, and a careful inspection can reveal creased shingles that are easy to miss from the ground.</p>

<h3>Alamo</h3>
<p>On June 12, 2025, a <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1277250" target="_blank" rel="noopener">strong microburst descended near North Alamo</a>, damaging traffic lights, power poles, fences, and other structures, with estimated gusts around 82 mph. Microbursts are localized, so neighboring properties can see very different damage. That is one reason a property-specific inspection matters.</p>

<h3>Hidalgo</h3>
<p>The city of Hidalgo sits on the Rio Grande south of McAllen. On June 30, 2011, a <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=307559" target="_blank" rel="noopener">short-lived EF1 tornado touched down in a subdivision about 4 miles east-northeast of Hidalgo</a>. Tornadoes are less common here than hail or straight-line wind, but they do occur in Hidalgo County.</p>

<h3>Weslaco</h3>
<p>Weslaco has experienced several damaging wind events. On April 24, 2015, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=574868" target="_blank" rel="noopener">microburst winds of 70 to 80 mph caused a large swath of damage across the Weslaco area</a>, including a newly built metal building that lost its front wall. A June 1, 2005 F0 tornado also caused roof damage to homes and a warehouse. Metal buildings and garage doors deserve close attention after wind events.</p>

<h3>Donna</h3>
<p>In Donna, law enforcement reported several power poles and lines down from estimated 81 mph winds on May 29, 2002, and a landspout was reported there on August 26, 2024. Donna also saw damage during the April 28, 2023 wind event. Wind-related claims here may also involve fallen trees and damaged outbuildings.</p>

<h3>Mercedes</h3>
<p>Mercedes has recorded two brief tornadoes in NCEI data: an F0 on June 1, 2005 that uprooted trees, one of which fell on a home, and an EF0 on September 2, 2007 that toppled trees near Texas and Tenth Streets. Tree-impact claims raise their own questions about debris removal and coverage limits, which we review against the policy.</p>

<h3>Other Hidalgo County communities</h3>
<p>We also serve Palmview, Peñitas, La Joya, Alton, Elsa, Edcouch, Progreso, and other communities across the county. Western Hidalgo County was hit especially hard on April 21, 2023, when <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1084560" target="_blank" rel="noopener">golf ball to baseball size hail fell across the Peñitas area</a>, and a week later on April 28, 2023, estimated 86 mph winds destroyed a mobile home near Peñitas. In La Joya, an August 31, 2001 straight-line wind event rolled RVs and a mobile home. Near Edcouch, a small tornado on March 27, 2025 lifted the sheet metal roof of a warehouse. Progreso was among the areas damaged on April 28, 2023. Many of these communities include manufactured homes, rural properties, and agricultural buildings, each with its own policy forms and coverage questions.</p>

